Here are the core concepts, senior-level interview questions, and a practical coding reference for Annotation-based Container Configuration.

📝 Main Points to Note Down

• Core Purpose: Shifts bean configuration metadata directly from detached files (like XML) into class bytecode using standard Java annotations, processed via internal BeanPostProcessor engines.
• Key Injection Annotations:
	• @Autowired: Drives type-based injection. Can be applied to constructors, fields, setter methods, or arbitrary multi-argument configuration methods.
	• @Qualifier: Resolves type ambiguity by filtering candidates using a specific text label or bean ID matching rule.
	• @Primary: Designates a default bean candidate to be injected automatically when multiple components match the target type.
• JSR-250 & JSR-330 Standard Support: Spring natively supports standard Java injection specifications alongside its own proprietary annotations:
	• JSR-250: Includes @Resource (which injects by name first, falling back to type).
	• JSR-330: Includes @Inject (maps directly to @Autowired) and @Named (maps to @Component / @Qualifier).
• The Single-Constructor Benefit: As of Spring 4.3, if a class defines exactly one constructor, you can omit the @Autowired annotation entirely. The container automatically interprets it as an intentional injection point.

🧠 Senior-Level Questions & Answers


Q: How does Spring resolve dependencies under the hood when a field is annotated with @Autowired vs. @Resource? Map out their lookup priority pathways.

• Answer: The two annotations utilize entirely separate internal post-processors, creating different lookup hierarchies:
	• @Autowired (processed via AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor): Resolves by-type first. If multiple beans share that type, it checks for a @Primary bean. If none exists, it falls back to a by-name check, matching the property or variable name against bean IDs. If that fails, it throws a NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException.
	• @Resource (processed via CommonAnnotationBeanPostProcessor): Resolves by-name first. It checks for a bean ID matching the explicitly provided name attribute or the field name. If no bean matches that specific name, it drops down to a by-type fallback.

Q: Why is field-level injection (@Autowired private MyService service;) heavily discouraged in modern, enterprise-grade architecture? What are the top three drawbacks?

• Answer: Field injection is widely considered an architectural anti-pattern for three main reasons:
	1. Breaks Immutability: You cannot declare fields as final, meaning dependencies can be inadvertently modified or re-assigned post-initialization.
	2. Violates Encapsulation & Testability: The class cannot be instantiated outside a running IoC container or integration test framework without relying on invasive reflection utilities (like ReflectionTestUtils) to manually set fields.
	3. Hides God-Class Smells: Constructor injection makes a high count of dependencies immediately noticeable in the constructor signature, signaling a violation of the Single Responsibility Principle. Field injection hides this bloating behind a clean layout of annotations.

Q: Imagine an interface PaymentGateway has three implementation beans. If you autowire a List<PaymentGateway> into a manager component, does Spring fail with an ambiguity exception? How can you control the ordering of elements inside that collection?

• Answer: No, Spring will not fail. When autowiring an array, List, or Map collection type, the container automatically bypasses single-value ambiguity checks. It collects all registered beans matching that interface or base class type and injects them into the collection.
• To control the ordering of components within that list, you decorate the implementation classes with Spring's @Order annotation or implement the Ordered interface. The container evaluates these metadata ranks during collection sorting, prioritizing lower integer values first.

💻 Practical Coding Reference: Advanced Annotation Layouts

This example demonstrates fallback mechanisms, custom qualifiers, and handling lists with specific injection priorities.
java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.AnnotationConfigApplicationContext;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Primary;
import org.springframework.core.annotation.Order;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

import jakarta.annotation.Resource;
import java.util.List;

@Configuration
public class AnnotationConfigDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var context = new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(AnnotationConfigDemo.class, 
                SmsNotifier.class, EmailNotifier.class, PushNotifier.class, NotificationManager.class);
        
        NotificationManager manager = context.getBean(NotificationManager.class);
        manager.processAlert("System Maintenance Scheduled");
        
        context.close();
    }
}

// --- Implementations with Ordering Strategy ---

interface Notifier { void send(String msg); }

@Component("sms")
@Order(2) // Lower value = higher priority in collections
class SmsNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String msg) { System.out.println("[SMS] " + msg); }
}

@Component("email")
@Primary // Acts as default choice for single-valued type lookups
@Order(1)
class EmailNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String msg) { System.out.println("[Email] " + msg); }
}

@Component("push")
@Order(3)
class PushNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String msg) { System.out.println("[Push] " + msg); }
}

// --- Consuming Component ---

@Component
class NotificationManager {

    // 1. Single-valued lookup: Resolves to EmailNotifier due to @Primary
    private final Notifier defaultNotifier;
    
    // 2. Collection Injection: Gathers all implementations, ordered automatically by @Order [Email, Sms, Push]
    private final List<Notifier> activeChannels;

    // 3. By-Name Explicit Routing using JSR-250 @Resource instead of @Autowired+@Qualifier
    @Resource(name = "push")
    private Notifier fallbackChannel;

    // Type-safe constructor injection (No @Autowired required on single constructor)
    public NotificationManager(Notifier defaultNotifier, List<Notifier> activeChannels) {
        this.defaultNotifier = defaultNotifier;
        this.activeChannels = activeChannels;
    }

    public void processAlert(String alertText) {
        System.out.println("--- Triggering Default ---");
        defaultNotifier.send(alertText);

        System.out.println("--- Triggering All Ordered Broadcasts ---");
        activeChannels.forEach(channel -> channel.send(alertText));

        System.out.println("--- Triggering Name-Routed Fallback ---");
        fallbackChannel.send(alertText);
    }
}
